WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.556
Kubernetes and orchestration context can
change the meaning of a container finding.

2
00:00:08.636 --> 00:00:15.910
A pod, deployment, service, namespace,
service account, admission policy,

3
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:22.256
network policy, and security context all
influence runtime risk.

4
00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:28.714
The same image may be lower or higher risk
depending on where it runs,

5
00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:34.836
what it can reach, and what identity or
permissions it receives.

6
00:00:34.916 --> 00:00:41.742
Security contexts can define user IDs,
privilege escalation settings,

7
00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:49.542
capabilities, seccomp profiles, AppArmor
or SELinux options where applicable,

8
00:00:49.622 --> 00:00:53.091
and read-only filesystem behavior.

9
00:00:53.171 --> 00:00:59.885
RBAC controls what a service account can
do through the Kubernetes API.

10
00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:05.448
Network policies can limit which workloads
communicate.

11
00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:12.130
Admission controls can prevent risky
workloads from being deployed.

12
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:19.259
These settings help turn a container
review into a deployment risk review.

13
00:01:19.339 --> 00:01:24.150
Image pull policies, registry controls,
signing,

14
00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.811
and promotion workflows also matter.

15
00:01:27.891 --> 00:01:33.710
If a cluster can pull mutable tags from an
untrusted registry,

16
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.042
the deployment may be harder to reason
about.

17
00:01:38.122 --> 00:01:44.947
If admission controls require signed
images from approved registries,

18
00:01:45.027 --> 00:01:49.167
the organization has stronger guardrails.

19
00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:56.185
Reviewers should connect image provenance
and build evidence to what the

20
00:01:56.265 --> 00:01:59.286
orchestrator actually allows.

21
00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:06.192
AI can help summarize manifests and map
findings to deployment context.

22
00:02:06.272 --> 00:02:12.314
It can identify questions about service
accounts, namespaces,

23
00:02:12.394 --> 00:02:17.429
network exposure, security contexts, and
policies.

24
00:02:17.509 --> 00:02:25.566
The reviewer still needs to verify the
live or approved configuration and understand

25
00:02:25.646 --> 00:02:28.219
operational constraints.

26
00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:36.020
Kubernetes context does not replace image
review; it completes the risk picture.
